[gd_scene load_steps=22 format=3]

; TRIPOD WORK LIGHT — see src/fidelity/work_light.gd for the design argument.
;
; This file owns the HARDWARE: the tripod, the column, the head, the guard, the
; lens and the tuned cone. The script owns everything that is derived from an
; export — the cable route, the battery block, the toolbox, the floor scuff.
;
; Numbers worth not changing casually:
;   legs at 0 / 137 / 244 degrees. Not 120 apart. A tripod set down by a person
;   is never symmetric, and symmetry is the single fastest way to make a hero
;   prop read as an asset. The struts lean 25.09 degrees, which is exactly
;   atan(0.44 / 0.94) — the geometry of a 0.44 m foot radius under a 0.94 m hub.
;
;   the head sits at 1.53 m. Eye height for the standing crew avatar is ~1.65,
;   so the lamp is just below the player's eyeline: you look slightly DOWN at
;   the source. A practical above eye height glares into the camera and the
;   player stops being able to see the room it is lighting.
;
;   the guard is in front of the lens, so the guard's own bars cross the cone
;   and land in the beam as real shadows on top of whatever gobo is loaded. That
;   doubling is free and it is most of why the pool of light reads as coming out
;   of a physical object.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://src/fidelity/work_light.gd" id="1_script"]
[ext_resource type="Material" path="res://assets/materials/fidelity/mat_brushed_steel.tres" id="2_steel"]
[ext_resource type="Material" path="res://assets/materials/fidelity/mat_hazard_band.tres" id="3_hazard"]
[ext_resource type="Texture2D" path="res://assets/gobos/gobo_vent_slat.png" id="4_gobo"]

